  legalFrameworkPakistan:
    "Pakistan’s labour framework is made up of multiple statutes rather than one universal employee-rights code. Pakistan Code currently lists core laws such as the Factories Act, 1934, the Minimum Wages Ordinance, 1961, the Industrial Relations Act, 2012, the Employees’ Old-Age Benefits Act, 1976, and the Employment of Children Act, 1991. Pakistan Code also separately lists the West Pakistan Shops and Establishments Ordinance, 1969. In Punjab, the Labour and Human Resource Department also publishes province-level labour laws, including the Punjab Occupational Safety and Health Act, 2019, and official minimum-wage notifications. \n\nFor workers, this means rights can arise from different legal routes. Wage-related issues may link to minimum-wage and payment obligations. Union and collective representation issues may fall under industrial-relations law. Safety concerns may fall under factory or occupational safety law. Pension and retirement-linked benefits can involve EOBI. Medical, injury, maternity, and related benefits may involve provincial social security institutions such as PESSI.\n\nThis is why a proper legal assessment matters. A worker may have a good grievance, but still lose time by going to the wrong office, using the wrong language, or asserting the wrong legal basis.",

