  mistakesTitle: "Common Mistakes and Legal Risks",
  commonMistakesAndRisks:
    "The first big mistake is assuming registration and filing are the same thing. They are linked, but they are not identical. A taxpayer may register and still fail to file correctly.\n\nThe second mistake is focusing only on declared income while ignoring wealth statement consistency. Many taxpayers understate the importance of asset and liability disclosure until the mismatch becomes visible.\n\nAnother common mistake is filing in panic near deadlines without checking bank records, withholding details, expenses, asset position, or entity-specific issues. That rushed approach often creates later rectification or explanation problems.\n\nBusinesses commonly make additional mistakes by failing to align accounting period, business activity description, ownership record, and tax posture. Individuals often rely too heavily on verbal advice or copied filing patterns from friends who are in entirely different tax situations.\n\nThe risks can include wrong declarations, weak explanations of assets, avoidable notices, ATL exclusion, higher tax cost in certain transactions, reputational damage, and long-term compliance instability.",

