    "From a lawyer’s perspective, the most dangerous property buyers are not dishonest buyers. They are overconfident buyers.\n\nA seasoned property lawyer does more than read a paper and say it looks fine. He asks questions that ordinary buyers usually miss. Is the title chain commercially clean, not just technically present? Is the seller the only competent transferor? Is the transaction structure exposing the buyer to future challenge? Is the agreement wording actually protecting the buyer’s money if the seller defaults? Is the property worth fighting for if a defect appears tomorrow?\n\nTiming is also critical. Legal review should happen before serious payment, not after. Once money changes hands informally, leverage usually weakens. If there is even a small sign of inheritance conflict, attorney misuse, unclear possession, old dispute, or record mismatch, the buyer should slow down, not speed up.",

