  commonMistakesAndRisks:
    "The first major mistake is copying templates without matching them to the real transaction.\n\nThe second mistake is using vague language because the parties want to avoid uncomfortable discussion at the start. Unfortunately, what is avoided in conversation usually returns as dispute.\n\nAnother common mistake is leaving payment, acceptance, confidentiality, and default consequences too weak or too generic. Businesses often discover later that the contract says a lot of formal words but very little that helps with recovery.\n\nPeople also make avoidable mistakes by signing without checking authority, failing to attach annexures, ignoring notice clauses, overlooking stamp or execution formalities where relevant, and assuming that a signed document automatically guarantees an easy case.\n\nThe risks include delayed payment, denial of obligations, evidentiary confusion, breach without practical remedy, confidentiality loss, commercial deadlock, and expensive litigation.",

