    "The first major mistake is assuming that truth alone is enough without documents, procedure, and timing.\n\nThe second mistake is hiding weak facts from the lawyer. A client who conceals damaging details usually harms the case more than the opponent does.\n\nAnother common mistake is appearing in court without understanding what the hearing is actually for. Many dates are not final-argument dates. Some are for notice, reply, objections, maintainability, evidence, bail confirmation, or other procedural stages.\n\nClients also make avoidable mistakes by not preserving originals, disobeying interim orders, speaking recklessly to the other side, posting carelessly on social media, or confusing legal advice with emotional reassurance.\n\nThe risks include adverse orders, missed relief, procedural setbacks, credibility loss, unnecessary delay, weak evidence posture, and avoidable damage to the client’s legal position.",

