    "Sales tax registration is the formal process through which a person liable under the federal sales tax system becomes a registered person for sales tax purposes. In practical terms, it allows the business to operate within the registered framework, issue compliant tax documentation where required, access filing systems, and deal with input-output tax logic subject to law.\n\nFBR’s official sales tax basics page explains that federal sales tax is levied under the Sales Tax Act, 1990 on the sale and supply of goods and on imported goods. That is important because many businesses loosely use the phrase 'sales tax' without first checking whether their activity concerns goods, imports, or a different provincial tax-on-services regime. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\n\nThis is where confusion usually starts. Some businesses assume all tax is the same. It is not. Before registration, the business should understand whether it is entering the federal sales tax-on-goods framework and whether its category creates a registration requirement under FBR’s current rules. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}",

